iatory  tariffs shake up the entire sort of  status quo of the economic relationships  of the United States with other  countries there's no undoing that now by  the same flip of a switch  we need negotiations we need meetings we  need cooperation agreements with other  countries it is going to take a long  time if at all under the Trump  administration and it may not happen  until later to undo the damage that he  so quickly did with those tariffs so we  do have to start talking about 2020 now  and I'm not doing horse race stuff I'm  not actually saying I'm now pretending  that we are in full 2020 election mode  but there are a few important things I  want to mention number one I believe  that Bernie Sanders is going to run for  president on the Democratic side I don't  want to say more than should be said  about it right now but there are people  I have spoken to personally who are in  positions where they can reliably talk  about this issue and there is a  significant group of these individuals  who are placed in a way that they could  say it with some certitude that Bernie  Sanders is going to be running for the  Democratic nomination for president in  2020 how that relates to whether other  progressives rather than just Democrats  run is something that needs to be  thought of already Bernie Sanders runs  and Elizabeth Warren runs what happens  is that what what are the dynamics of  that in the context of as the two most  progressive members of the Senate  comparing and contrasting them to a more  sort of establishment centrist figure  that's going to become an issue from  what I am told pretty soon pretty early  in 2019 that is going to be a thing that  we are going to be talking about and  discussing in 2020 either Donald Trump  will be running for re-election in which  case the goal is that we have two years  now to weaken him as much as possible  taking over the House of Representatives  is a good first step in achieving that  as long as and this is the risk